lavapipe and the Vulkan runtime saying what they have, as Khronos's conformance suite reads it
(docs/gpu3d.md G2):

- VK_EXT_headless_surface, VK_EXT_hdr_metadata and
  VK_EXT_multisampled_render_to_swapchain were advertised whatever the
  build, and they need VK_KHR_surface and VK_KHR_swapchain,
  which come only with a window platform. Kosmos's lavapipe has none yet,
  and dEQP-VK.info's extension_dependencies cases fail an implementation
  that lists an extension without what it depends on; with this, each is
  listed with its dependency or not at all.
- VK_EXT_memory_budget's heapUsage is "how much memory the process is
  currently using in that heap"; lavapipe answered with the whole machine's
  used memory, which moves when the allocator maps pages rather than when
  the process allocates. On Kosmos it is what this process's devices hold,
  counted where they allocate and free - so a second instance in the same
  process sees the first's, as dEQP-VK.info.device_memory_budget_multi_instance
  holds it to.
- vkCreateInstance answers VK_ERROR_LAYER_NOT_PRESENT for a layer that is
  not there. On Linux the loader does, and the driver never sees the name;
  on Kosmos the Vulkan runtime is the whole of Vulkan - one driver, linked
  in, no loader and no layers - so it refuses every layer itself, as
  dEQP-VK.api.device_init.create_instance_layer_name_abuse holds it to.
  And the loader's other answers, for the same reason: vkDestroyDevice
  with no device does nothing, as vkDestroyInstance with no instance
  already did here (dEQP-VK.api.null_handle.destroy_device read through
  the null pointer), and asking for the layers into an array of any size
  is VK_SUCCESS and a count of none - the driver's lavapipe and the
  runtime's answered VK_ERROR_LAYER_NOT_PRESENT.

